# harness-config-push managed allowlist v1
# =============================================================================
# Generated by `harness-config-push` -- DO NOT EDIT.
# Per-store rules go in HARNESS_CONFIG_INCLUDE / HARNESS_CONFIG_EXCLUDE, which
# are rendered into the marked block near the bottom. Hand-editing this file
# forks it, and a fork drifts: the 277-line original this was generalized from
# grew that way, one `auto: ignore ...` commit at a time, always AFTER something
# had already been committed and pushed.
#
# WHAT THIS KEEPS (the durable signal):
#   sessions/                       Hermes conversation history
#   webui/sessions/*.json           WebUI chat sessions
#   home/.claude/projects/*.jsonl   Claude Code CLI chat history
#   home/.codex/sessions/*.jsonl    Codex CLI chat history
#   user-skills/, scripts/          hand-authored plumbing
#   config.yaml, SOUL.md, memories/, cron/jobs.json
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE is ignored: bundled skills, plugins, runtime locks, caches,
# checkpoints, state DBs -- all regenerable, or carried by the restic snapshot
# (`harness-backup`) rather than here. This repo TRACKS CONFIG; it is not the
# disaster-recovery copy, and it deliberately holds no credentials.
#
# ALLOWLIST, not denylist, and that direction is load-bearing. It used to be the
# other way round and the denylist kept losing: every new stray at the repo root
# got committed and PUSHED by the 6-hourly `git add -A` before anyone noticed.
# Worse, $HERMES_HOME was $HOME for the in-process WebUI agent, so agent scratch
# landed here by default and got published. An allowlist inverts the default: a
# new root entry is invisible unless someone names it. Adding a keep is one line;
# forgetting one costs a missed file, which is recoverable. The old default cost
# a publish, which is not.
#
# NOTE this does NOT untrack anything -- git ignores .gitignore for files already
# in the index. It governs only what appears NEXT. Untracking is `git rm --cached`
# and harness-config-push will never do it for you.
# =============================================================================

# --- Root allowlist ----------------------------------------------------------
# Ignore everything at the root, then re-open only durable state. Deeper rules
# below still apply INSIDE these -- `!/home/` merely opens the directory so the
# home/ partial allowlist can do its work. A parent must be re-included before
# any child can be, which is why directories appear here even when only a
# fraction of their contents is kept.
/*
!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/SOUL.md
!/config.yaml
!/cron/
!/home/
!/memories/
!/plugins/
!/scripts/
!/sessions/
!/user-skills/
!/webui/

# --- Ephemeral runtime state -------------------------------------------------
# Everything here mutates on a timer rather than on a decision. Tracking any of
# it makes every backup interval produce a diff, which is how a wake-gate that
# exists to detect real change ends up firing constantly and getting ignored.
.update_check
.skills_prompt_snapshot.json
.clean_shutdown
.session_cleanup.lock
.codex_gpt55_autoraise_notice
.gws_write_approval.json
.npm_lock_hash_*
context_length_cache.yaml
processes.json
gateway.pid
gateway.lock
gateway_state.json
gateway/
terminal-sessions/
state/
whatsapp/
pairing/
slack-manifest.json
pastes/
web-ui-build-stamp.json
*.init.lock
*.dispatch.lock

# SQLite stores and their sidecars. The restic snapshot carries these via
# `hermes backup`, which copies them with sqlite3.backup() -- a consistent image
# even under a live writer. A file-level git copy cannot do that: it would pair
# a main file with stale WAL state and produce a torn restore.
state.db
response_store.db
verification_evidence.db
kanban.db
kanban/
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite-shm
*.sqlite-wal

# Provider/model discovery caches -- regenerated on demand
models_dev_cache.json
models_dev_cache.etag
*_models_cache.json

# Logs & history
logs/
.hermes_history
*.log
.local/share/tirith/

# Backup/rollback blobs -- local safety copies that grow on every edit
config.yaml.bak*
config.yaml.*.bak
config.yaml.corrupt.*
.curator_backups/

# Cron internals. cron/jobs.json IS tracked -- durable job definitions worth
# versioning -- but every telemetry field beside it mutates each tick.
cron/output/
cron/.tick.lock
cron/.jobs.lock
cron/.fire-*.lock
cron/**/*.log
cron/ticker_heartbeat
cron/ticker_last_success
cron/executions.db
cron/notepad.db*
cron/usage_audit.jsonl

# Caches & temp
audio_cache/
image_cache/
*_cache/
cache/
tmp/
.npm/
node/
bin/
lsp/

# Bundled trees -- regenerable via `hermes update`. Personal skills live under
# user-skills/ (kept above).
skills/
sandboxes/
checkpoints/
state-snapshots/

# Plugins: the directory is opened by the root allowlist so a store can re-include
# a specific hand-authored override via HARNESS_CONFIG_INCLUDE. Nothing is kept
# by default -- bundled plugins are reinstallable.
plugins/*

# Debug request dumps -- captured by the retry/error path. Duplicate content
# already in sessions/, and leak request bodies verbatim.
sessions/request_dump_*.json

# WebUI. Allowlist, not denylist, and the correction is worth the words: this block used to
# open `webui/` wholesale and then name the noise files to exclude. A second store had
# `webui/.pbkdf2_key` and `webui/.signing_key` -- 32 bytes each, mode 600, the password-hashing
# and session-signing keys -- plus `.sessions.json`, `.login_attempts.json` and `shares/`. None
# were in the noise list, so all of them staged on the first dry run against that store. The
# first store had none of them, which is exactly why enumerating noise fails: it can only ever
# describe the files that existed when it was written.
#
# So: close webui/ and re-open only the chat record.
webui/*
!webui/sessions/
webui/sessions/*
!webui/sessions/*.json
!webui/attachments/
# The journals duplicate the canonical chat record, churn on every turn, and carry no
# portability value.
webui/sessions/_index.json
webui/sessions/_run_journal/
webui/sessions/_turn_journal/
webui/sessions/*.bak*
webui-recovery/
channel_directory.json

# Secondary Hermes profiles -- each is structurally its own Hermes home (own
# sessions/, memories/, auth.json, config.yaml). A profile belongs to whoever
# operates it and should run its own backup, not piggy-back on this repo.
profiles/

# --- home/ partial allowlist -------------------------------------------------
# $HERMES_HOME/home is the agent's `~`. Nothing under it is tracked except the
# CLI chat histories, which are the point.
home/*
home/.*/
!home/.claude/
home/.claude/*
!home/.claude/projects/
!home/.codex/
home/.codex/*
!home/.codex/sessions/
home/.cache/
home/.npm/

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/

# --- Per-store rules ---------------------------------------------------------
# Rendered from HARNESS_CONFIG_INCLUDE (as `!/<entry>`) and
# HARNESS_CONFIG_EXCLUDE (verbatim). Deliberately ABOVE the secret tail so that
# nothing a store adds can re-open a credential.
@HARNESS_CONFIG_EXTENSIONS@

# --- Secret tail. ALWAYS LAST. -----------------------------------------------
# Last rule wins in gitignore, so these denies outrank every re-include above,
# including anything a store added. The original this came from placed them near
# the TOP and relied on "an earlier deny on a specific filename beats a later
# un-ignore of its parent directory" -- which is true, but only holds while
# nobody writes an explicit re-include for the filename itself. Putting them last
# makes the guarantee unconditional rather than incidental.
#
# harness-config-push ALSO refuses to commit these by path, and that redundancy
# is deliberate: this file can be forked, but the guard ships in the image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
auth.json
auth.lock
.git-credentials
.netrc
.npmrc
google_token.json
google_client_secret.json
google_oauth_pending.json
google_oauth_last_url.txt
mcp-tokens/
home/.config/gh/hosts.yml
# Backup credentials. rclone.conf holds an OAuth REFRESH token for the backup destination, and
# .restic-password decrypts every snapshot -- between them they are full read/write access to
# the disaster-recovery copy. Both are only incidentally caught by the broad rules above (a root
# entry, and home/*); naming them is the point of this block.
rclone.conf
home/.config/rclone/
.restic-password
.restic-password.*
home/.claude/.credentials.json
home/.codex/auth.json
home/.gemini/oauth_creds.json
home/.gemini/google_accounts.json
home/.gemini/antigravity-cli/antigravity-oauth-token
*.pem
id_rsa
id_ed25519
# Key material by name as well as by directory. The webui block above closes the directory
# these were found in; these lines survive a store re-opening it.
*.key
*_key
.signing_key
.pbkdf2_key
